WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:26.400
In 1963, when it became independent from the
British Empire and after its economy was devastated

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.480
by the Japanese occupation during the Second
World War, Singapore was nothing more than

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.279
an agglomeration of shanties.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:36.390
A place where every bathroom and kitchen was
shared by several families .

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:47.440
Things at the economic and social level were
hard, and they weren’t very different at

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.489
the political level either.

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:52.250
The truth is that Singapore’s beginnings
weren’t at all promising:

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:59.510
See, at first, Singapore united with Malaysia,
but the romance didn’t last long.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.840
Just two years later, in 1965, and after several
political and ethnic clashes with the aristocratic

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.680
and extractive Malaysian government, Singapore
was expelled from the Malay Federation and

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.000
was de facto forced to declare itself independent.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.810
Folks, the challenge that this small territory
faced was huge:

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.680
Singapore was poor, it had less than two million
inhabitants, and it lacked any natural resources.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.620
It wasn’t even self-sufficient in terms
of food and water!

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:37.829
But just a second, because the story we all
know is a bit different, don’t you think?

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:42.310
Today, just five decades later Singapore is
an independent country, and this city-state

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:47.430
is one of the capitals of the world and one
of the richest territories on the planet.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.680
Check out the following graph.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:02:00.790
Folks, take a seat: since 1960, Singapore's
economy has grown by more than 7.5 percent

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.229
per year.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:07.200
We’re talking about a huge growth rate that
has allowed Singapore to experience the fastest

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.910
development process in the history of mankind.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:15.190
Today this city’s per capita income is much
higher than that of the United States, it

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:20.110
is more than twice that of middle-income countries
like Spain, and almost four times that of

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.220
Chile, which is considered the most prosperous
Latin American country.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.680
Don’t tell me that isn’t amazing.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:36.260
Now, the question we can all ask ourselves
is, how did this tiny city-state achieve such

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.530
a huge amount of economic growth?

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:47.350
Well, let's see.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.629
(TAKE-OFF)

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:58.500
Folks, this entire story has one clear protagonist:
Lee Kuan Yew the autocratic Prime Minister

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:09.310
who governed Singapore between 1959 and 1990.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:15.650
Now, few people would’ve bet that this country
would end up having a bright future.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.500
And, folks, Lee Kuan Yew’s first years leading
Singapore didn’t bode well.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.390
As Secretary-General of the Popular Action
Party, a party that belonged to the socialist

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:29.120
international party, this Prime Minister initially
flirted with so-called import substitution

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.450
industrialization policies.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:37.080
A political approach that blindly commits
to protectionism as a way to achieve development.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.099
Something that was particularly popular throughout
the 20th century in Latin America.

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:45.380
Well, actually, in a way, it still is.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:52.569
Really, in his first years of government,
Lee Kuan Yew was closer to Argentine politicians

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:55.420
than to anyone who supported the free market
and globalization.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.680
However, folks, unlike what happened in Latin
America, in Singapore, they soon realized

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.280
that this type of policy was by no means the
best way to escape poverty.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:14.670
That's why, starting in 1965, a very different
approach was launched-- a political and economic

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.160
commitment that transformed Singapore completely:

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.700
Opening up to the global market.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.370
“When most Third World countries denounced
the exploitation of Western multinationals,

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.270
in Singapore we invited them.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:30.960
That’s how we achieved growth, technologies
and the know-how that boosted our productivity

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.909
more than any other economic policy could’ve
done.”

00:04:33.909 --> 00:04:35.160
Lee Kuan Yew)

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.840
Now, Singapore hasn’t been anything close
to a prototype of an orthodox libertarian

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.140
state, where the government doesn’t interfere
in any economic activity.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.710
On the contrary, the Singaporean government
has played, and still plays a very important

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.710
role.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.149
But before you attack me, let's say they did
it in a very different way than we’re used

00:04:52.149 --> 00:04:55.430
to, for example, in old Europe.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.710
“Things are changing quickly and we’re
having to adjust and keep up; due to technology,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:04.500
globalization, we’ve had to anticipate what’s
happening, we have to help companies and workers

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.500
to adapt.”

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:06.719
Lee Hsien Loong)

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:10.590
In Singapore, the government didn’t try
to take over the larger sections of the economy;

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:13.529
nor did they attempt to regulate everything
down to the smallest detail;

00:05:13.529 --> 00:05:20.500
nor, of course, did they try to expand public
spending and taxes.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:21.820
Quite the opposite.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:26.520
In Singapore’s case, we can basically think
of it as a state-company or, better yet, a

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.730
city-company.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:36.610
That is, Lee Kuan Yew understood that Singapore
needed to attract capital and professionals

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:46.210
so that the country could grow and develop;
And within an imperfect competitive environment

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:50.150
between economies, and the heavy intervention
that exists in the countries we live in, the

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.870
state can play a key role.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:56.250
And it can do so with policies that always
favor competitiveness, investment, and the

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:04.980
attraction of companies, instead of bureaucracy
or welfare.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:12.830
That’s why the Singaporean authorities developed
institutions to guarantee legal security and

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.599
efficient management of the city;
they promoted economic sectors through public-private

00:06:17.599 --> 00:06:22.020
collaboration, drew up an ambitious productive
infrastructure program and, most importantly,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.230
promoted savings.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:27.599
And that’s not all, all policies were designed
within a strict budget stability framework

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:32.910
and always, always with the mindset of integrating
them with private agents, something that rarely

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:40.849
happens in countries like Spain, Italy or,
of course, Argentina.

00:06:40.849 --> 00:06:49.210
All right, allow me to explain.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:53.460
It isn’t as much about expanding a port
or a road but being able to integrate an attractive

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.640
institutional framework with services that
cause companies to settle in a country, and

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:01.080
to invest in order to increase their productivity.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.550
Let’s put it another way: How do companies
compete in the market?

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:13.849
By offering better conditions than the competition,
right?

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:18.710
Well Lee Kuan Yew understood that in our world
competition between countries isn’t very

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:19.710
different.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:22.979
Therefore, perhaps the best way to see and
understand the keys to Singapore's success

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:27.240
is to view this city-state not as a country
to be used, but as a service provider in a

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.240
competitive industry.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:32.070
In Singapore, the government understood that
the community itself, the city itself, was

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:38.750
an asset and that, well managed, it could
become a competitive advantage.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:45.300
But to understand this better, let’s take
a look at some concrete examples of the policies

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.470
that Lee Kuan Yew set on the table:

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:51.380
First objective: despite having a city with
a very high population density, turn it into

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.600
a non-chaotic, unnoisy, and unpolluted place.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.520
To do this, the government imposed traffic
taxes in 1975, so today traffic in the city

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:05.209
is subject to tolls that are charged electronically
to all users on the road-- that is, to all

00:08:05.209 --> 00:08:06.209
vehicles.

00:08:06.209 --> 00:08:09.520
That way, they managed to impute the streets’
maintenance costs to the real road users,

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:15.659
managed to avoid traffic jams, and avoided
a big cause of pollution.

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:19.430
The result of this approach has been that
today Singapore, despite having a very high

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:24.140
population density and being one of the economic
engines of the world, has very fluid traffic

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:28.880
and, in addition, imposes maintenance costs
based on each driver’s use of the road.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.780
That is, if the benefit you gain isn’t greater
than your toll, which is the maintenance cost

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:34.510
of the road itself, then you leave the car
at home.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:39.880
And if your benefit is greater, then you have
roads through which you can move quickly.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.950
Smart, isn’t it?

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:46.920
Of course, to make all this possible, Singapore
developed a fantastic, very efficient and

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:52.000
affordable public transportation system.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.520
Another much more controversial measure due
to its paternalism, is forcing all workers

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.160
to invest a quarter of their salary into a
rigid national savings plan that’s also

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:02.180
managed by a state organization.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:06.140
With this program, the island’s government
gets workers to accumulate savings with which

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:10.180
they can pay for health services, educational
services and their own retirement.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:14.210
And while that happens, the state gets the
capital it needs to invest in projects, in

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:18.580
and out of the country, that are understood
as essential for the city’s competitiveness.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:23.270
In a certain way, workers don’t only become
users but also investors in this city-company.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:27.710
.
Of course these types of “interventions”

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.970
where the state maintains clear leadership,
are complemented by a policy that favors free

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:37.360
enterprise and one of the freest regulations
in the world in terms of capital, goods, and

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.400
worker circulation.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:45.110
Folks, in Singapore, taxes are low – for
example, someone with a 60 thousand dollar

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:48.770
income pays 5 percent income tax -;
tariffs are practically non-existent,

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:52.150
there’s no minimum wage;
public expenditure accounts for approximately

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:56.550
18% of the GDP, and if all that weren’t
enough, Singapore’s economy is considered

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.780
to be the second freest in the world!

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:04.440
“See, in the world we live in today, being
peripheral or being central doesn’t depend

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.740
on your country’s physical location, it
depends on whether or not you have a room

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.720
and an economy of knowledge.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.450
It depends on technology, it’s the only
thing that can explain why countries like

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:17.990
Singapore have a much better education than
us.”

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:23.860
Albert Rivera, MP of Spain)

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:28.090
The result of all this is that a country that,
until a few decades ago, was very poor, is

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.820
now one of the richest countries in the world.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.490
And don’t think that it’s just a matter
of economic numbers.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:37.970
Singapore also has some of the best universities,
with students at the forefront of subjects

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:42.210
like science and mathematics, their healthcare
system is considered excellent, there’s

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:49.130
no unemployment, there’s no crime, and their
expectations for the future are extraordinary.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:53.680
For example, in the Human Development Index
produced by the United Nations, Singapore

00:10:53.680 --> 00:11:01.110
occupies the tenth place, above countries
like Sweden, Iceland, France, and Finland.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:05.930
The negative side--and there is one--is concentrated
in its political system, which, although it’s

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:09.270
evolving, is still very restrictive of civil
liberties.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:13.340
In Singapore, prison sentences are very high,
censorship is a normal part of life, and different

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.240
behaviors aren’t usually well-seen.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:21.421
We’ll talk about this and more in future
videos, because, of course, Singapore is one

00:11:21.421 --> 00:11:25.900
of the most fascinating countries in the world
and a real magnet for professionals from all

00:11:25.900 --> 00:12:36.840
over

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:59.870
the globe.

